{
  "review_id": "V4-IR-e8141bbc41",
  "stage": "SCENE_IR",
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "issues": [
    {
      "issue_id": "ISSUE-LIGASE-ENDPOINTS",
      "severity": "blocker",
      "description": "Ligase seals the nick between gap_fill and fragment_1, but these two DNA objects have overlapping/extending geometry (gap_fill end coincident with fragment_1 start at x=-1.2, yet gap_fill was extended from -0.6 to -1.2 while fragment_1 extends from -1.2 to -3.5). The connection is between the correct biology endpoints, but the ligase is attached to gap_fill at its growing 3' end rather than at the actual nick between gap_fill and fragment_1, and no target anchor pairing (gap_fill.end ↔ fragment_1.start) is visibly established by a common junction. The covalent seal 'connect' transitions use anchor pairs whose coincidence is asserted by the deterministic checker but the drawn geometry shows the two strands merged into a single pass-through line; the sealing junction is not depicted as a true nick being closed.",
      "root_cause_stage": "SCENE_IR",
      "target_objects": [
        "gap_fill",
        "fragment_1",
        "ligase"
      ],
      "target_events": [
        "event_5_maturation_maturation_11",
        "event_5_maturation_maturation_12"
      ],
      "repair_scope": "Re-anchor the ligase active site to the gap_fill.end ↔ fragment_1.start nick and ensure the sealed_nick relation is created exactly at that junction, with both DNA segments meeting end-to-end (not overlapping) so the seal is a visible nick closure.",
      "acceptance_criteria": [
        "ligase attaches to the shared endpoint of gap_fill.end and fragment_1.start, not gap_fill.center or growing end.",
        "gap_fill.end and fragment_1.start are coincident within tolerance 0.03 before sealing and remain a single contiguous strand after sealing; no overhang or overlap is drawn.",
        "The sealed_nick relation is between exactly gap_fill → fragment_1 at those anchors."
      ]
    },
    {
      "issue_id": "ISSUE-PRIMASE-OVERLAP-LAG",
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "In event_4, primase is shown synthesizing the lagging-strand primer rna_primer_lagging_1 centered at [-0.9, -0.386] and rna_primer_lagging_2 at [1.4, -0.064], but these visual centers sit off the template_lagging strand (y=-0.95 for the paired region). RNA primers are objects on the template, so their schematic position should be adjacent to their template footprints at y≈-0.95, not floating above/below the strand by ~0.56–0.89 units. The primer_extension_continuity relations bind primer 'start'/'end' to fragment 'start', but the primer polylines are drawn at y=-0.95 while object centers used for the primase binding are at y=-0.386/-0.064, which is ambiguous about which primer footprint is being extended.",
      "root_cause_stage": "SCENE_IR",
      "target_objects": [
        "primase",
        "rna_primer_lagging_1",
        "rna_primer_lagging_2"
      ],
      "target_events": [
        "event_4_two_modes_fragment_synthesis_0",
        "event_4_two_modes_fragment_synthesis_11"
      ],
      "repair_scope": "Keep the primase enzyme center offset above the strand for readability, but place the RNA primer polylines on the template_lagging strand at the footprint of the new fragment (y≈-0.95) so primer_extension_continuity endpoints are geometrically genuine adjacent endpoints.",
      "acceptance_criteria": [
        "rna_primer_lagging_1 points lie on/near template_lagging (y within 0.05 of the strand) at the fragment_1 footprint x-range, and rna_primer_lagging_2 similarly at the fragment_2 footprint.",
        "primer_extension_continuity relation between primer end and fragment start uses genuine adjacent endpoints (measured distance ≤0.03)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "issue_id": "ISSUE-MATURATION-POL-BINDING",
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "During maturation, Pol delta is shown extending gap_fill (event_5_maturation_maturation_4–7), but the gap_fill object's initial, very short geometry [[-0.6,-0.95],[-0.62,-0.95]] and its growth toward x=-1.2 overlaps the region already occupied by the rna_primer_lagging_1 footprint (which was removed at maturation_1). The primer removal step happens before Pol delta extension, which matches the allowed abstraction ('individual RNaseH/FEN1 steps omitted'), but the removal is instant with no shown replacement synthesis from a primer 3' OH. This is a minor representational simplification rather than a science error, but it makes 'remove RNA, fill DNA, then seal' visually ambiguous about where the 3' OH that primes gap_fill comes from.",
      "root_cause_stage": "SCENE_IR",
      "target_objects": [
        "gap_fill",
        "rna_primer_lagging_1",
        "polymerase_lagging"
      ],
      "target_events": [
        "event_5_maturation_maturation_1",
        "event_5_maturation_maturation_3"
      ],
      "repair_scope": "Add a short caption/note or visual state indicating that after primer removal the DNA is extended from the adjacent fragment_2 3' end (by Pol delta), so gap_fill grows from fragment_2.end toward fragment_1.start; the current initial gap_fill seed at x=-0.6 already sits at the downstream fragment_2 end, which is consistent, so primarily clarify the priming 3' OH origin.",
      "acceptance_criteria": [
        "The maturation caption/annotation states gap_fill is extended from the fragment_2 3' OH toward fragment_1.",
        "gap_fill.start remains coincident with fragment_2.end (≤0.03) throughout maturation."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "notes": [
    "Deterministic checks (polarity, growth direction, event order, topology, active-site bindings) all PASS; template/new-strand polarity is consistent with the verified 5'→3' antiparallel rule.",
    "Role labels (Pol epsilon leading, Pol delta lagging, RNA primase, helicase schematic) match the common teaching model and F4–F6; Pol epsilon framing is correctly hedged as model-level in simplifications.",
    "Primer 3' extension (new_leading growing away from the original primer end) is consistent with the allowed simplification note about covalently extended primer 3' moving away from the primer, not staying adjacent.",
    "No telomerase/chromosome-end claims; single local fork; one local maturation only — within declared teaching scope. No demand for RNaseH/FEN1 molecular steps."
  ],
